First, let me summarize what’s going on:

•  You have a single “boundary” tile at zoom 0 whose world‐extent is slightly *smaller* in one direction than your fixed pixel tile grid.  As a result your 1D data array no longer exactly matches “width×height×bands” under WebGL’s default row‐alignment assumptions and you get the classic

    WebGL warning: texImage: Desired upload requires more data than is available…

•  By default WebGL expects each scanline to be padded so that its start address is a multiple of 4 bytes (“UNPACK_ALIGNMENT = 4”).  Since your rows are not a multiple of 4‐bytes long, WebGL insists on extra padding and then complains that it doesn’t find enough data.

**Fix**  
Just before you call `gl.texImage2D` on pure array data, switch the pixel store alignment to 1:

    gl.pixelStorei(gl.UNPACK_ALIGNMENT, 1);

That tells WebGL “don’t insert any row padding, rows are tightly packed” and everything will upload correctly.

